<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cuyamel Fruit Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cuyamel Fruit Company was an American banana company founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-zemurray">Samuel Zemurray</a> in 1911, with plantations in the lower valleys of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> near the Guatemalan border. It competed fiercely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a> for land, railway concessions and influence over Honduran governments until Zemurray sold it to United Fruit in 1929.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuyamel-fruit-company.jpg" alt="Cuyamel Fruit Company" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Cuyamel Fruit Company Vector: Designism · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_flag_of_Cuyamel_Fruit_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The company grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-zemurray">Samuel Zemurray</a>'s banana trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, Zemurray began as a teenager in Mobile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, selling at a discount the bananas the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a> discarded for ripening early, through agents at major rail stops. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> to be near the docks and helped found a steamship line, the Hubbard-Zemurray Steam Ship Company, which imported bananas from independent growers and cut spoilage in transit. Before United Fruit grew bananas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> itself from 1910, it owned 60% of Zemurray's firm and half of its rival Vaccaro Brothers and Company, the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-fruit-company">Standard Fruit Company</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1910 President Miguel R. Dávila granted Vaccaro land for a railway and barred any competing line within 20 kilometres. Zemurray, who had already backed a failed coup against Dávila in 1908, supplied weapons and transport to former president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-bonilla">Manuel Bonilla</a>, whose coup drove Dávila out. In 1911 Zemurray turned his firm into the Cuyamel Fruit Company, acquired 5,000 acres along the Cuyamel River and, as reward for his support, received from Bonilla a railway concession inland from the town of Cuyamel. Its main Honduran office was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cortes">Puerto Cortés</a>.</p><p>The company's opening of land on the Guatemala-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> border almost brought war between the two states, averted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> mediation. In 1916 the American Embassy described Cuyamel's territory as a state within another state. Incorporated in Delaware, with its board meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, it worked through subsidiaries such as the Cortés Company and the Sula Sugar Company. In 1925 it issued $5 million in bonds to buy land along the Ulúa River, and in 1927 it supplied about 14% of the bananas sold in the United States.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the crash of October 1929 Zemurray sold the company to United Fruit for 300,000 shares, then worth $31 million, which secured United Fruit's hold on the American market. He later took control of United Fruit and ran it from 1933 to 1954. The coups of 1908 and 1911 began a tradition of banana companies intervening in Central American governments that lasted until the 1970s. Cuyamel and United Fruit are both corporate ancestors of Chiquita Brands International.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cuyamel Fruit Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive change came with bananas: from the 1890s the Vaccaro brothers, later Standard Fruit, Samuel Zemurray’s <b>Cuyamel Fruit Company</b> and, from 1912, United Fruit acquired vast concessions on the north coast in return for building railways, and by the late 1920s Honduras was the world’s leading banana…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
